

In the event of multiple GPU cards installed be sure to check the
bullshit NVidia "compute" level crap number which determines the
version of the internal PTX code base to build into the output
binary blob.

see https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-gpus

Where I need 3.5 and 5.0 and also 6.0

$ nvcc -ccbin /usr/bin/g++-10 -I../include -m64 \
-gencode arch=compute_35,code=sm_35 \
-gencode arch=compute_50,code=sm_50 \
-gencode arch=compute_60,code=sm_60 \
-Wno-deprecated-gpu-targets \
-lnppi_static -lculibos -c -o dev_info.o dev_info.cpp

$ nvcc -ccbin /usr/bin/g++-10 -I../include -m64 \
-gencode arch=compute_35,code=sm_35 \
-gencode arch=compute_50,code=sm_50 \
-gencode arch=compute_60,code=sm_60 \
-Wno-deprecated-gpu-targets -o dev_info dev_info.o 
$ 


------------------------------------------------------

It is difficult to navigate the CUDA docs to find out
what really exists in the device props struct. There are
conflicting pages. The key issue is managed memory and
perhaps the data exists ... or not .. as a trivial integer
flag.

See https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/11.8.0/cuda-runtime-api/structcudaDeviceProp.html#structcudaDeviceProp

